      "title": "A Negative Answer to a Question of Novak on Randomly Shifted Lattices and the Expected p-th Power of the L_p Discrepancy",
      "description": "<p>In an Oberwolfach report of 2004, E. Novak (joint work with A. Hinrichs) considered the generalized lattices with shift M_n^{z,\u0394} = {jz + \u0394 mod 1 : 0 \u2264 j &lt; n}, with z and \u0394 independent and uniformly distributed in [0,1]^d, and asked whether, for even p, the expectation of disc_p^p(M_n^{z,\u0394}), the p-th power of the L_p discrepancy, is at most the corresponding expectation for n independent uniformly distributed points. We show that the answer is negative. Equality holds for p = 2, because the lattice points are pairwise independent (in substance a known fact, which underlies random Weyl sampling), and for n \u2264 2. For n = 3 and p = 4 the difference of the two expectations is (8/9)\u00b72^(\u2212d)\u00b7((2/5)^d \u2212 (19/48)^d) &gt; 0 in every dimension d; for d = 1 the two values are 19/1620 and 16/1620. A closed formula shows that n = 4, p = 4 fails in every dimension as well. For every d and every even p \u2265 4 the inequality fails for all n \u2265 N(d,p), with N(d,p) explicit: with probability of order 1/n the points form a short arithmetic progression, which gives E disc_p^p \u2265 4/((p+1)^d (p+2)(p+3)(n\u22121)) for the lattices, against order n^(\u2212p/2) for independent points. For thirteen pairs (p,d), among them p = 4 with d \u2264 4, the inequality fails for every n \u2265 3; this part is computer-assisted (exact rational arithmetic and a certified polynomial inequality). We conjecture failure for all n \u2265 3, d \u2265 1 and even p \u2265 4. The answer concerns the p-th power, as in the source; for the expectation of disc_p itself, which the wording of the corpus record also allows, all computed cases go the other way. The results are asymptotic in n for fixed d and p and do not exclude bounds of the i.i.d. type when n grows polynomially with d. The question is Novak's; no answer to it was found in the literature accessible to us. This is an unrefereed note.</p><p>Unrefereed preprint released for independent mathematical scrutiny. Publication on Zenodo does not constitute peer review. AI-assisted tools supported research, computation, proof development, and manuscript preparation. The author remains responsible for all claims and the final text.</p><p>Corpus identifier: OWR-723-005 (Novak, New bounds for the star discrepancy, Oberwolfach Report 13/2004, pp. 696\u2013699).</p>",
